IMPORT prelude

-- Jurisdiction of Courts (Cross-vesting) Act 1987 (SA) -- THE RULES (seat-neutral core).
-- Every rule of the Act that a party to a civil proceeding meets, written from the deposited text section by section.
-- The courts' opinions and judgments ("it appears to the court", "more appropriate", "the interests of justice",
-- "special reasons") are taken as inputs and never decided here. The seat layer (jccv-seat.l4) asks the questions and
-- restates none of these rules.
--
-- Based on content from the South Australian Legislation website (version of 4 September 2006, fetched 6 November
-- 2026; the register's unauthorised RTF). CC BY 4.0. BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Jurisdiction of Courts (Cross-vesting) Act 1987 (SA): the rules`

§§ `ss 3(1), 3A, 4(5): what the Act covers`

-- s 3(1) "proceeding does not include a criminal proceeding"; s 4(5) no criminal jurisdiction; s 3A the Corporations
-- Act 2001 (Cth) Part 9.6A Division 1 jurisdiction is outside the Act.
DECLARE `Coverage answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: your proceeding is a civil proceeding, which the cross-vesting scheme covers (s 3(1))`
    `no: a criminal proceeding is not a proceeding under this Act, and the Act gives no court criminal jurisdiction (ss 3(1), 4(5))`
    `no: the Act does not apply to the jurisdiction dealt with by Division 1 of Part 9.6A of the Corporations Act 2001 (Cth) (s 3A)`

GIVEN `criminal` IS A BOOLEAN, `Corporations Part 9.6A` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Coverage answer`
`does the Act cover the proceeding (ss 3(1), 3A, 4(5))` `criminal` `Corporations Part 9.6A` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `criminal` THEN `no: a criminal proceeding is not a proceeding under this Act, and the Act gives no court criminal jurisdiction (ss 3(1), 4(5))`
           IF `Corporations Part 9.6A` THEN `no: the Act does not apply to the jurisdiction dealt with by Division 1 of Part 9.6A of the Corporations Act 2001 (Cth) (s 3A)`
           OTHERWISE `yes: your proceeding is a civil proceeding, which the cross-vesting scheme covers (s 3(1))`

§§ `ss 3(1), 4: State matters heard in other States' courts`

-- The courts the Act names. "State" includes the Northern Territory and the ACT; "Territory" excludes both (s 3(1)),
-- so a Territory's Supreme Court here is an external territory's (Norfolk Island's).
DECLARE `Court` IS ONE OF
    `the Supreme Court of South Australia`
    `a court of South Australia other than the Supreme Court`
    `the Supreme Court of another State, the Northern Territory or the ACT`
    `the Supreme Court of an external Territory`
    `the State Family Court of another State (the Family Court of Western Australia)`
    `the Federal Court of Australia`
    `the Family Court of Australia`
    `another court or tribunal`

-- s 3(1) "State matter means (a) a matter in which the Supreme Court has jurisdiction otherwise than by reason of a law
-- of the Commonwealth or of another State; or (b) a matter removed to the Supreme Court under section 8".
GIVEN `SA Supreme Court has it under SA law` IS A BOOLEAN, `removed under s 8` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is a State matter (s 3(1))` `SA Supreme Court has it under SA law` `removed under s 8` IF
        `SA Supreme Court has it under SA law`
    OR  `removed under s 8`

DECLARE `State matter jurisdiction answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the Supreme Court of another State or of a Territory has and may exercise original and appellate jurisdiction in your South Australian matter (s 4(3))`
    `yes: the State Family Court of another State has and may exercise original and appellate jurisdiction in your South Australian matter (s 4(4))`
    `no: your matter is not a State matter, so s 4 vests no jurisdiction in it (ss 3(1), 4)`
    `no: s 4 vests no jurisdiction with respect to criminal matters (s 4(5))`
    `no: the Act does not apply to Corporations Act Part 9.6A Division 1 jurisdiction (s 3A)`
    `s 4 is not needed: the Supreme Court of South Australia has jurisdiction in a State matter of its own (s 3(1))`
    `not covered: s 4 now vests jurisdiction only in other States' and Territories' Supreme Courts and State Family Courts (its subsections (1) and (2) were deleted in 2001); this Act does not say`

GIVEN `court` IS A `Court`, `State matter` IS A BOOLEAN, `criminal` IS A BOOLEAN, `Corporations Part 9.6A` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `State matter jurisdiction answer`
`may that court hear the State matter (s 4)` `court` `State matter` `criminal` `Corporations Part 9.6A` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `criminal` THEN `no: s 4 vests no jurisdiction with respect to criminal matters (s 4(5))`
           IF `Corporations Part 9.6A` THEN `no: the Act does not apply to Corporations Act Part 9.6A Division 1 jurisdiction (s 3A)`
           IF `court` EQUALS `the Supreme Court of South Australia` THEN `s 4 is not needed: the Supreme Court of South Australia has jurisdiction in a State matter of its own (s 3(1))`
           IF (NOT `State matter`) THEN `no: your matter is not a State matter, so s 4 vests no jurisdiction in it (ss 3(1), 4)`
           IF `court` EQUALS `the Supreme Court of another State, the Northern Territory or the ACT` THEN `yes: the Supreme Court of another State or of a Territory has and may exercise original and appellate jurisdiction in your South Australian matter (s 4(3))`
           IF `court` EQUALS `the Supreme Court of an external Territory` THEN `yes: the Supreme Court of another State or of a Territory has and may exercise original and appellate jurisdiction in your South Australian matter (s 4(3))`
           IF `court` EQUALS `the State Family Court of another State (the Family Court of Western Australia)` THEN `yes: the State Family Court of another State has and may exercise original and appellate jurisdiction in your South Australian matter (s 4(4))`
           OTHERWISE `not covered: s 4 now vests jurisdiction only in other States' and Territories' Supreme Courts and State Family Courts (its subsections (1) and (2) were deleted in 2001); this Act does not say`

§§ `s 5: transfer of proceedings`

DECLARE `Federal court` IS ONE OF `the Federal Court`, `the Family Court`

DECLARE `Transfer answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the Supreme Court must transfer your proceeding to the Federal Court (s 5(1))`
    `yes: the Supreme Court must transfer your proceeding to the Family Court (s 5(1))`
    `not required: s 5(1) does not require the Supreme Court to transfer a federal matter proceeding while the related State matter proceeding is pending in a South Australian court, but it may if it considers that appropriate in the interests of justice (s 6A(4))`
    `yes: the court must transfer your proceeding to the other Supreme Court, as it arises out of or is related to a proceeding there and that court is more appropriate (s 5(2)(b)(i))`
    `yes: the court must transfer your proceeding to the other Supreme Court, as more appropriate having regard to where it could be brought, the law it turns on and the interests of justice (s 5(2)(b)(ii))`
    `yes: the court must transfer your proceeding to the other Supreme Court in the interests of justice (s 5(2)(b)(iii))`
    `yes: the other Supreme Court must transfer your proceeding to the Supreme Court of South Australia, as it arises out of or is related to a proceeding there and that court is more appropriate (s 5(3)(b)(i))`
    `yes: the other Supreme Court must transfer your proceeding to the Supreme Court of South Australia, as more appropriate having regard to where it could be brought, the law it turns on and the interests of justice (s 5(3)(b)(ii))`
    `yes: the other Supreme Court must transfer your proceeding to the Supreme Court of South Australia in the interests of justice (s 5(3)(b)(iii))`
    `yes: the Federal Court or Family Court must transfer your proceeding to the Supreme Court of South Australia, as it arises out of or is related to a proceeding there and that court is more appropriate (s 5(4)(b)(i))`
    `yes: the Federal Court or Family Court must transfer your proceeding to the Supreme Court of South Australia, as more appropriate having regard to where it could be brought, the law it turns on and the interests of justice (s 5(4)(b)(ii))`
    `yes: the Federal Court or Family Court must transfer your proceeding to the Supreme Court of South Australia in the interests of justice (s 5(4)(b)(iii))`
    `s 5(4) does not apply to a federal matter proceeding; the Federal Court or Family Court may still transfer it to the Supreme Court of South Australia in the interests of justice (ss 6A(2), (3))`
    `no: the court has not formed the view that s 5 requires, so s 5 does not require a transfer`

-- s 5(1): pending in the SA Supreme Court; it appears to that court, having regard to (A), (B) and (C), more
-- appropriate that the Federal Court or the Family Court determine it. s 6A(4)(a) lifts the duty for a federal
-- matter proceeding whose State matter proceeding is pending in any court of this State.
GIVEN `more appropriate there` IS A BOOLEAN, `which` IS A `Federal court`, `s 6A(4) case` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Transfer answer`
`transfer to the Federal Court or Family Court (s 5(1))` `more appropriate there` `which` `s 6A(4) case` MEANS
    BRANCH IF (NOT `more appropriate there`) THEN `no: the court has not formed the view that s 5 requires, so s 5 does not require a transfer`
           IF `s 6A(4) case` THEN `not required: s 5(1) does not require the Supreme Court to transfer a federal matter proceeding while the related State matter proceeding is pending in a South Australian court, but it may if it considers that appropriate in the interests of justice (s 6A(4))`
           IF `which` EQUALS `the Federal Court` THEN `yes: the Supreme Court must transfer your proceeding to the Federal Court (s 5(1))`
           OTHERWISE `yes: the Supreme Court must transfer your proceeding to the Family Court (s 5(1))`

-- s 5(2): pending in the SA Supreme Court ("the first court"); any of (i), (ii), (iii) as it appears to that court.
GIVEN `related and more appropriate` IS A BOOLEAN, `more appropriate on the factors` IS A BOOLEAN, `otherwise in the interests of justice` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Transfer answer`
`transfer to another Supreme Court (s 5(2))` `related and more appropriate` `more appropriate on the factors` `otherwise in the interests of justice` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `related and more appropriate` THEN `yes: the court must transfer your proceeding to the other Supreme Court, as it arises out of or is related to a proceeding there and that court is more appropriate (s 5(2)(b)(i))`
           IF `more appropriate on the factors` THEN `yes: the court must transfer your proceeding to the other Supreme Court, as more appropriate having regard to where it could be brought, the law it turns on and the interests of justice (s 5(2)(b)(ii))`
           IF `otherwise in the interests of justice` THEN `yes: the court must transfer your proceeding to the other Supreme Court in the interests of justice (s 5(2)(b)(iii))`
           OTHERWISE `no: the court has not formed the view that s 5 requires, so s 5 does not require a transfer`

-- s 5(3): pending in the Supreme Court of another State or of a Territory; transfer to the SA Supreme Court.
GIVEN `related and more appropriate` IS A BOOLEAN, `more appropriate on the factors` IS A BOOLEAN, `otherwise in the interests of justice` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Transfer answer`
`transfer to the Supreme Court of South Australia from another Supreme Court (s 5(3))` `related and more appropriate` `more appropriate on the factors` `otherwise in the interests of justice` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `related and more appropriate` THEN `yes: the other Supreme Court must transfer your proceeding to the Supreme Court of South Australia, as it arises out of or is related to a proceeding there and that court is more appropriate (s 5(3)(b)(i))`
           IF `more appropriate on the factors` THEN `yes: the other Supreme Court must transfer your proceeding to the Supreme Court of South Australia, as more appropriate having regard to where it could be brought, the law it turns on and the interests of justice (s 5(3)(b)(ii))`
           IF `otherwise in the interests of justice` THEN `yes: the other Supreme Court must transfer your proceeding to the Supreme Court of South Australia in the interests of justice (s 5(3)(b)(iii))`
           OTHERWISE `no: the court has not formed the view that s 5 requires, so s 5 does not require a transfer`

-- s 5(4): pending in the Federal Court or the Family Court; transfer to the SA Supreme Court. s 6A(3): s 5(4) does
-- not apply to a federal matter proceeding (s 6A(2) gives a discretion instead).
GIVEN `federal matter proceeding` IS A BOOLEAN, `related and more appropriate` IS A BOOLEAN, `more appropriate on the factors` IS A BOOLEAN, `otherwise in the interests of justice` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Transfer answer`
`transfer to the Supreme Court of South Australia from the Federal Court or Family Court (s 5(4))` `federal matter proceeding` `related and more appropriate` `more appropriate on the factors` `otherwise in the interests of justice` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `federal matter proceeding` THEN `s 5(4) does not apply to a federal matter proceeding; the Federal Court or Family Court may still transfer it to the Supreme Court of South Australia in the interests of justice (ss 6A(2), (3))`
           IF `related and more appropriate` THEN `yes: the Federal Court or Family Court must transfer your proceeding to the Supreme Court of South Australia, as it arises out of or is related to a proceeding there and that court is more appropriate (s 5(4)(b)(i))`
           IF `more appropriate on the factors` THEN `yes: the Federal Court or Family Court must transfer your proceeding to the Supreme Court of South Australia, as more appropriate having regard to where it could be brought, the law it turns on and the interests of justice (s 5(4)(b)(ii))`
           IF `otherwise in the interests of justice` THEN `yes: the Federal Court or Family Court must transfer your proceeding to the Supreme Court of South Australia in the interests of justice (s 5(4)(b)(iii))`
           OTHERWISE `no: the court has not formed the view that s 5 requires, so s 5 does not require a transfer`

-- s 5(6): a related proceeding follows a transferred one.
DECLARE `Related transfer answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the first court must transfer your related proceeding to the same court as the one it transferred (s 5(6))`
    `no: s 5(6) applies only where the court has transferred another proceeding under a cross-vesting law, yours is related to it and pending in that court, and it finds the interests of justice favour the other court`

GIVEN `other transferred` IS A BOOLEAN, `mine related and pending in the first court` IS A BOOLEAN, `interests of justice` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Related transfer answer`
`must the related proceeding follow (s 5(6))` `other transferred` `mine related and pending in the first court` `interests of justice` MEANS
    IF      `other transferred`
        AND `mine related and pending in the first court`
        AND `interests of justice`
    THEN `yes: the first court must transfer your related proceeding to the same court as the one it transferred (s 5(6))`
    ELSE `no: s 5(6) applies only where the court has transferred another proceeding under a cross-vesting law, yours is related to it and pending in that court, and it finds the interests of justice favour the other court`

-- s 5(7): who may set a transfer going. s 3(1): "party ... includes a person who intervenes in the proceeding".
DECLARE `Applicant` IS ONE OF
    `a party to the proceeding`
    `a person who has intervened in the proceeding`
    `the Attorney-General of the Commonwealth, a State or a Territory`
    `someone else`

DECLARE `Who may apply answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: as a party you may apply for the transfer; the court may also act on its own initiative (ss 3(1), 5(7))`
    `yes: an intervener is a party, so you may apply for the transfer (ss 3(1), 5(7))`
    `yes: an Attorney-General may apply for the transfer (s 5(7))`
    `the Act gives you no application: s 5(7) names a party, an Attorney-General and the court of its own initiative; the court may still transfer on its own initiative`

GIVEN `who` IS AN `Applicant`
GIVETH A `Who may apply answer`
`who may apply for a transfer (s 5(7))` `who` MEANS
    CONSIDER `who`
    WHEN `a party to the proceeding` THEN `yes: as a party you may apply for the transfer; the court may also act on its own initiative (ss 3(1), 5(7))`
    WHEN `a person who has intervened in the proceeding` THEN `yes: an intervener is a party, so you may apply for the transfer (ss 3(1), 5(7))`
    WHEN `the Attorney-General of the Commonwealth, a State or a Territory` THEN `yes: an Attorney-General may apply for the transfer (s 5(7))`
    OTHERWISE `the Act gives you no application: s 5(7) names a party, an Attorney-General and the court of its own initiative; the court may still transfer on its own initiative`

-- s 5(8): the lawyer's right of audience follows the transfer.
DECLARE `Right to practise answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: your lawyer has the same right to practise in the new court in this proceeding as in a federal court exercising federal jurisdiction (s 5(8))`
    `s 5(8) does not help: it carries over only the entitlement of a barrister or solicitor entitled to practise in the court the proceeding left, and only for the transferred proceeding and a related one determined with it`

GIVEN `entitled in the old court` IS A BOOLEAN, `the transferred proceeding or one determined with it` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Right to practise answer`
`may the lawyer practise in the new court (s 5(8))` `entitled in the old court` `the transferred proceeding or one determined with it` MEANS
    IF `entitled in the old court` AND `the transferred proceeding or one determined with it`
    THEN `yes: your lawyer has the same right to practise in the new court in this proceeding as in a federal court exercising federal jurisdiction (s 5(8))`
    ELSE `s 5(8) does not help: it carries over only the entitlement of a barrister or solicitor entitled to practise in the court the proceeding left, and only for the transferred proceeding and a related one determined with it`

§§ `s 6: special federal matters`

-- s 3(1) "special federal matter" has the meaning in the Commonwealth Act s 3(1); its paragraphs (a)-(e) go to the
-- Federal Court and (ab) to the Family Court, the Family Court of Western Australia or the NT Supreme Court (s 6(2)).
DECLARE `Special federal matter` IS ONE OF
    `not a special federal matter`
    `a special federal matter under paragraph (a), (b), (c), (d) or (e) of the Commonwealth definition`
    `a special federal matter under paragraph (ab) of the Commonwealth definition`

DECLARE `Special federal matter answer` IS ONE OF
    `s 6 does not apply: no matter for determination is a special federal matter`
    `s 6 does not apply: an appeal in the Full Court where the court below ordered under s 6(3) that it determine the special federal matter (s 6(9))`
    `the Supreme Court keeps it: it has ordered under s 6(3) that it determine the proceeding`
    `not required: s 6 does not require the Supreme Court to transfer a federal matter proceeding while the related State matter proceeding is pending in a South Australian court, but it may if it considers that appropriate in the interests of justice (s 6A(4))`
    `yes: the Supreme Court must transfer so much of your proceeding as is within the Federal Court's jurisdiction (including its accrued jurisdiction) to the Federal Court (s 6(1), (1a), (2)(a))`
    `yes: the Supreme Court must transfer so much of your proceeding as is within that court's jurisdiction to whichever of the Family Court, the Family Court of Western Australia or the Supreme Court of the Northern Territory it considers appropriate (s 6(1), (1a), (2)(b))`

GIVEN `matter` IS A `Special federal matter`, `appeal after a s 6(3) order below` IS A BOOLEAN, `s 6(3) order made` IS A BOOLEAN, `s 6A(4) case` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Special federal matter answer`
`must the special federal matter be transferred (s 6)` `matter` `appeal after a s 6(3) order below` `s 6(3) order made` `s 6A(4) case` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `matter` EQUALS `not a special federal matter` THEN `s 6 does not apply: no matter for determination is a special federal matter`
           IF `appeal after a s 6(3) order below` THEN `s 6 does not apply: an appeal in the Full Court where the court below ordered under s 6(3) that it determine the special federal matter (s 6(9))`
           IF `s 6(3) order made` THEN `the Supreme Court keeps it: it has ordered under s 6(3) that it determine the proceeding`
           IF `s 6A(4) case` THEN `not required: s 6 does not require the Supreme Court to transfer a federal matter proceeding while the related State matter proceeding is pending in a South Australian court, but it may if it considers that appropriate in the interests of justice (s 6A(4))`
           IF `matter` EQUALS `a special federal matter under paragraph (ab) of the Commonwealth definition` THEN `yes: the Supreme Court must transfer so much of your proceeding as is within that court's jurisdiction to whichever of the Family Court, the Family Court of Western Australia or the Supreme Court of the Northern Territory it considers appropriate (s 6(1), (1a), (2)(b))`
           OTHERWISE `yes: the Supreme Court must transfer so much of your proceeding as is within the Federal Court's jurisdiction (including its accrued jurisdiction) to the Federal Court (s 6(1), (1a), (2)(a))`

-- s 6(3), (4), (5): the order to keep a special federal matter.
DECLARE `Keep order answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the Supreme Court may order that it determine your proceeding itself (s 6(3), (4))`
    `not yet: a written notice of the nature of the special federal matter must first be given to the Commonwealth and South Australian Attorneys-General; the court may adjourn and may direct a party to give it (s 6(4)(a), (5))`
    `not yet: a reasonable time must first pass after the notice for the Attorneys-General to consider making submissions; the court may adjourn for it (s 6(4)(b), (5)(a))`
    `no: reasons relevant to the convenience of the parties are not special reasons (s 6(3))`
    `no: the court may keep it only if satisfied that there are special reasons in the particular circumstances (s 6(3))`

GIVEN `special reasons` IS A BOOLEAN, `only convenience` IS A BOOLEAN, `notice given to both` IS A BOOLEAN, `reasonable time passed` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Keep order answer`
`may the Supreme Court keep the special federal matter (s 6(3)-(5))` `special reasons` `only convenience` `notice given to both` `reasonable time passed` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `only convenience` THEN `no: reasons relevant to the convenience of the parties are not special reasons (s 6(3))`
           IF (NOT `special reasons`) THEN `no: the court may keep it only if satisfied that there are special reasons in the particular circumstances (s 6(3))`
           IF (NOT `notice given to both`) THEN `not yet: a written notice of the nature of the special federal matter must first be given to the Commonwealth and South Australian Attorneys-General; the court may adjourn and may direct a party to give it (s 6(4)(a), (5))`
           IF (NOT `reasonable time passed`) THEN `not yet: a reasonable time must first pass after the notice for the Attorneys-General to consider making submissions; the court may adjourn for it (s 6(4)(b), (5)(a))`
           OTHERWISE `yes: the Supreme Court may order that it determine your proceeding itself (s 6(3), (4))`

-- s 6(7): urgent interlocutory relief.
DECLARE `Urgent relief answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: nothing in s 6 stops the Supreme Court granting urgent interlocutory relief in the interests of justice (s 6(7))`
    `s 6(7) does not help: it saves only urgent relief of an interlocutory nature that is in the interests of justice; this Act does not say more`

GIVEN `urgent and interlocutory` IS A BOOLEAN, `interests of justice` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Urgent relief answer`
`may urgent relief be granted meanwhile (s 6(7))` `urgent and interlocutory` `interests of justice` MEANS
    IF `urgent and interlocutory` AND `interests of justice`
    THEN `yes: nothing in s 6 stops the Supreme Court granting urgent interlocutory relief in the interests of justice (s 6(7))`
    ELSE `s 6(7) does not help: it saves only urgent relief of an interlocutory nature that is in the interests of justice; this Act does not say more`

-- ss 6(8), 7(7): a decision made in the wrong court is not invalidated.
DECLARE `What went wrong` IS ONE OF
    `the Supreme Court decided a special federal matter without a s 6(3) order or the s 6(4) notice, through inadvertence`
    `the Full Court of the Supreme Court decided an appeal that s 7(4) sends elsewhere, having found the interests of justice required it to`
    `the Full Court of the Supreme Court decided an appeal that s 7(4) sends elsewhere, through inadvertence`
    `something else`

DECLARE `Validity answer` IS ONE OF
    `no: nothing in s 6 invalidates the decision (s 6(8))`
    `no: nothing in s 7 invalidates the decision (s 7(7))`
    `not covered: ss 6(8) and 7(7) save only those decisions; this Act does not say`

GIVEN `what` IS A `What went wrong`
GIVETH A `Validity answer`
`is the decision invalidated (ss 6(8), 7(7))` `what` MEANS
    CONSIDER `what`
    WHEN `the Supreme Court decided a special federal matter without a s 6(3) order or the s 6(4) notice, through inadvertence` THEN `no: nothing in s 6 invalidates the decision (s 6(8))`
    WHEN `the Full Court of the Supreme Court decided an appeal that s 7(4) sends elsewhere, having found the interests of justice required it to` THEN `no: nothing in s 7 invalidates the decision (s 7(7))`
    WHEN `the Full Court of the Supreme Court decided an appeal that s 7(4) sends elsewhere, through inadvertence` THEN `no: nothing in s 7 invalidates the decision (s 7(7))`
    OTHERWISE `not covered: ss 6(8) and 7(7) save only those decisions; this Act does not say`

§§ `s 6A: Commonwealth authorities or officers acting under State laws`

-- s 6A(1)(a)-(c): the "federal matter proceeding".
GIVEN `paragraph (c) or (e) matter` IS A BOOLEAN, `Commonwealth body under a State enactment` IS A BOOLEAN, `related State matter proceeding pending in SA` IS A BOOLEAN, `State proceeding arises under the State enactment` IS A BOOLEAN, `State proceeding has no (c) or (e) matter` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is a federal matter proceeding (s 6A(1))` `paragraph (c) or (e) matter` `Commonwealth body under a State enactment` `related State matter proceeding pending in SA` `State proceeding arises under the State enactment` `State proceeding has no (c) or (e) matter` IF
        `paragraph (c) or (e) matter`
    AND `Commonwealth body under a State enactment`
    AND `related State matter proceeding pending in SA`
    AND `State proceeding arises under the State enactment`
    AND `State proceeding has no (c) or (e) matter`

DECLARE `Federal matter proceeding answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes, a federal matter proceeding: the Federal Court or Family Court may transfer it to the Supreme Court of South Australia in the interests of justice, including the desirability of related proceedings being heard together; s 5(4) does not apply (s 6A(2), (3))`
    `yes, a federal matter proceeding: the Supreme Court of South Australia need not transfer it to the Federal Court or Family Court under s 5(1) or s 6, but may if it considers that appropriate (s 6A(4))`
    `yes, a federal matter proceeding, but s 6A says what follows only while it is in the Federal Court, the Family Court or the Supreme Court of South Australia`
    `no: s 6A applies only to a proceeding that meets all of s 6A(1)(a), (b) and (c)`

GIVEN `federal matter proceeding` IS A BOOLEAN, `where` IS A `Court`
GIVETH A `Federal matter proceeding answer`
`what follows for a federal matter proceeding (s 6A)` `federal matter proceeding` `where` MEANS
    BRANCH IF (NOT `federal matter proceeding`) THEN `no: s 6A applies only to a proceeding that meets all of s 6A(1)(a), (b) and (c)`
           IF `where` EQUALS `the Federal Court of Australia` THEN `yes, a federal matter proceeding: the Federal Court or Family Court may transfer it to the Supreme Court of South Australia in the interests of justice, including the desirability of related proceedings being heard together; s 5(4) does not apply (s 6A(2), (3))`
           IF `where` EQUALS `the Family Court of Australia` THEN `yes, a federal matter proceeding: the Federal Court or Family Court may transfer it to the Supreme Court of South Australia in the interests of justice, including the desirability of related proceedings being heard together; s 5(4) does not apply (s 6A(2), (3))`
           IF `where` EQUALS `the Supreme Court of South Australia` THEN `yes, a federal matter proceeding: the Supreme Court of South Australia need not transfer it to the Federal Court or Family Court under s 5(1) or s 6, but may if it considers that appropriate (s 6A(4))`
           OTHERWISE `yes, a federal matter proceeding, but s 6A says what follows only while it is in the Federal Court, the Family Court or the Supreme Court of South Australia`

§§ `s 7: appeals`

-- s 7: the Schedule is the Schedule to the Commonwealth Act (federal Acts such as the Trade Practices Act 1974 and
-- the Family Law Act 1975, as it stood); whether a matter arises under one is asked.
DECLARE `Decision appealed from` IS ONE OF
    `a single judge of the Supreme Court of South Australia`
    `a single judge of the Federal Court or the Family Court`
    `a judge of a State Family Court`
    `another court`

DECLARE `Appeal route answer` IS ONE OF
    `to the Full Court of the Supreme Court of South Australia, and only there (s 7(3))`
    `to the Full Court of the Federal Court or of the Family Court, as the case requires, or with special leave to the High Court, and only there (s 7(4)), subject to s 7(6) and (7)`
    `no appeal lies from a single judge of the Federal Court or the Family Court to the Full Court of the Supreme Court of South Australia, nor from one of those courts to the other (s 7(1), (2)); this Act does not say where it goes`
    `the appeal may be dealt with as if no matter arose under a Schedule Act (s 7(5))`
    `not covered: s 7 does not say where this appeal goes`

GIVEN `from` IS A `Decision appealed from`, `Schedule Act matter` IS A BOOLEAN, `another matter too` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH AN `Appeal route answer`
`where does the appeal go (s 7)` `from` `Schedule Act matter` `another matter too` MEANS
    CONSIDER `from`
    WHEN `a single judge of the Supreme Court of South Australia` THEN
        IF `Schedule Act matter`
        THEN `to the Full Court of the Federal Court or of the Family Court, as the case requires, or with special leave to the High Court, and only there (s 7(4)), subject to s 7(6) and (7)`
        ELSE `to the Full Court of the Supreme Court of South Australia, and only there (s 7(3))`
    WHEN `a single judge of the Federal Court or the Family Court` THEN `no appeal lies from a single judge of the Federal Court or the Family Court to the Full Court of the Supreme Court of South Australia, nor from one of those courts to the other (s 7(1), (2)); this Act does not say where it goes`
    WHEN `a judge of a State Family Court` THEN
        IF `Schedule Act matter` AND `another matter too`
        THEN `the appeal may be dealt with as if no matter arose under a Schedule Act (s 7(5))`
        ELSE `not covered: s 7 does not say where this appeal goes`
    OTHERWISE `not covered: s 7 does not say where this appeal goes`

-- s 7(6): the Full Court of the Supreme Court finds part way through that s 7(4) applies.
DECLARE `Full Court transfer answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the Full Court must transfer your appeal to the Full Court of the Federal Court or of the Family Court, as the case requires (s 7(6))`
    `no: the interests of justice require the Full Court to go on and decide your appeal itself (s 7(6)); its decision is not invalid (s 7(7))`
    `no: s 7(6) applies only where the Full Court has begun hearing the appeal and it then appears that s 7(4) applies`

GIVEN `hearing begun` IS A BOOLEAN, `s 7(4) appears to apply` IS A BOOLEAN, `interests of justice require it to go on` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Full Court transfer answer`
`will the Full Court send the appeal on (s 7(6))` `hearing begun` `s 7(4) appears to apply` `interests of justice require it to go on` MEANS
    BRANCH IF (NOT (`hearing begun` AND `s 7(4) appears to apply`)) THEN `no: s 7(6) applies only where the Full Court has begun hearing the appeal and it then appears that s 7(4) applies`
           IF `interests of justice require it to go on` THEN `no: the interests of justice require the Full Court to go on and decide your appeal itself (s 7(6)); its decision is not invalid (s 7(7))`
           OTHERWISE `yes: the Full Court must transfer your appeal to the Full Court of the Federal Court or of the Family Court, as the case requires (s 7(6))`

§§ `s 8: removal into the Supreme Court`

DECLARE `Pending in` IS ONE OF
    `a South Australian court other than the Supreme Court`
    `a tribunal established by or under an Act`
    `somewhere else`

DECLARE `Removal answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: you may apply (or the Supreme Court may act of its own initiative) and the Supreme Court may remove your proceeding to itself; the Act then applies as if it were pending there, and the Supreme Court may later remit it (s 8)`
    `no: s 8 reaches only a proceeding pending in a South Australian court other than the Supreme Court, or in a tribunal established by or under an Act`
    `no: the Supreme Court may remove it only where it appears to that court that it is related to a proceeding in the Federal Court, the Family Court or another Supreme Court that could then be transferred to it, or that removal is needed to consider a transfer (s 8(1)(b))`

GIVEN `where` IS A `Pending in`, `related and that one could come here` IS A BOOLEAN, `needed to consider a transfer` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Removal answer`
`may the proceeding be removed to the Supreme Court (s 8)` `where` `related and that one could come here` `needed to consider a transfer` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `where` EQUALS `somewhere else` THEN `no: s 8 reaches only a proceeding pending in a South Australian court other than the Supreme Court, or in a tribunal established by or under an Act`
           IF `related and that one could come here` OR `needed to consider a transfer` THEN `yes: you may apply (or the Supreme Court may act of its own initiative) and the Supreme Court may remove your proceeding to itself; the Act then applies as if it were pending there, and the Supreme Court may later remit it (s 8)`
           OTHERWISE `no: the Supreme Court may remove it only where it appears to that court that it is related to a proceeding in the Federal Court, the Family Court or another Supreme Court that could then be transferred to it, or that removal is needed to consider a transfer (s 8(1)(b))`

§§ `s 10: Trade Practices Act Part V matters sent down`

DECLARE `Trade practices transfer answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: you may apply (or the court may act of its own initiative) and the Supreme Court may transfer your proceeding to the South Australian court that has jurisdiction over all of it (s 10)`
    `no: s 10 needs every one of its conditions: pending in a Supreme Court, a matter under Division 1 or 1A of Part V of the Trade Practices Act 1974, no special federal matter, not an appeal, and a lower South Australian court with jurisdiction over all the matters`

GIVEN `pending in a Supreme Court` IS A BOOLEAN, `TPA Part V Div 1 or 1A matter` IS A BOOLEAN, `a special federal matter` IS A BOOLEAN, `an appeal` IS A BOOLEAN, `a lower SA court has all of it` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Trade practices transfer answer`
`may the Trade Practices matter go to a lower court (s 10)` `pending in a Supreme Court` `TPA Part V Div 1 or 1A matter` `a special federal matter` `an appeal` `a lower SA court has all of it` MEANS
    IF      `pending in a Supreme Court`
        AND `TPA Part V Div 1 or 1A matter`
        AND (NOT `a special federal matter`)
        AND (NOT `an appeal`)
        AND `a lower SA court has all of it`
    THEN `yes: you may apply (or the court may act of its own initiative) and the Supreme Court may transfer your proceeding to the South Australian court that has jurisdiction over all of it (s 10)`
    ELSE `no: s 10 needs every one of its conditions: pending in a Supreme Court, a matter under Division 1 or 1A of Part V of the Trade Practices Act 1974, no special federal matter, not an appeal, and a lower South Australian court with jurisdiction over all the matters`

§§ `s 11: the law applied, and steps already taken`

DECLARE `Law applied answer` IS ONE OF
    `the written and unwritten law of the State or Territory under whose written law your right of action arises; rules of evidence and procedure as the court considers appropriate, from those of a superior court in Australia or an external Territory (s 11(1)(b), (c))`
    `the law in force in the State or Territory where the court sits, including its choice of law rules; rules of evidence and procedure as the court considers appropriate, from those of a superior court in Australia or an external Territory (s 11(1)(a), (c))`
    `s 11(1) does not direct the court: it applies only where the court will, or is likely to, be exercising cross-vested jurisdiction`

GIVEN `cross-vested jurisdiction` IS A BOOLEAN, `right of action under another State's written law` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Law applied answer`
`which law will the court apply (s 11(1))` `cross-vested jurisdiction` `right of action under another State's written law` MEANS
    BRANCH IF (NOT `cross-vested jurisdiction`) THEN `s 11(1) does not direct the court: it applies only where the court will, or is likely to, be exercising cross-vested jurisdiction`
           IF `right of action under another State's written law` THEN `the written and unwritten law of the State or Territory under whose written law your right of action arises; rules of evidence and procedure as the court considers appropriate, from those of a superior court in Australia or an external Territory (s 11(1)(b), (c))`
           OTHERWISE `the law in force in the State or Territory where the court sits, including its choice of law rules; rules of evidence and procedure as the court considers appropriate, from those of a superior court in Australia or an external Territory (s 11(1)(a), (c))`

DECLARE `Steps answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the new court deals with your proceeding as if the steps taken in the old court, including its orders, had been taken there (s 11(3))`
    `yes, except as the new court has ordered otherwise (s 11(3))`
    `s 11(3) does not apply: your proceeding has not been transferred or removed`

GIVEN `transferred or removed` IS A BOOLEAN, `new court ordered otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Steps answer`
`do the steps already taken count (s 11(3))` `transferred or removed` `new court ordered otherwise` MEANS
    BRANCH IF (NOT `transferred or removed`) THEN `s 11(3) does not apply: your proceeding has not been transferred or removed`
           IF `new court ordered otherwise` THEN `yes, except as the new court has ordered otherwise (s 11(3))`
           OTHERWISE `yes: the new court deals with your proceeding as if the steps taken in the old court, including its orders, had been taken there (s 11(3))`

§§ `ss 12, 13, 14: costs, no appeal, enforcement`

DECLARE `Costs answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the court your proceeding went to may order costs for the conduct of the proceeding before the transfer or removal (s 12)`
    `no: s 12 reaches only costs that another court has not already dealt with`
    `s 12 does not apply: your proceeding has not been transferred or removed`

GIVEN `transferred or removed` IS A BOOLEAN, `already dealt with` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Costs answer`
`may the new court order the earlier costs (s 12)` `transferred or removed` `already dealt with` MEANS
    BRANCH IF (NOT `transferred or removed`) THEN `s 12 does not apply: your proceeding has not been transferred or removed`
           IF `already dealt with` THEN `no: s 12 reaches only costs that another court has not already dealt with`
           OTHERWISE `yes: the court your proceeding went to may order costs for the conduct of the proceeding before the transfer or removal (s 12)`

DECLARE `Decision` IS ONE OF
    `a decision to transfer or remove the proceeding, or not to, under this Act`
    `a decision on which rules of evidence and procedure apply under s 11(1)`
    `another decision`

DECLARE `Appeal bar answer` IS ONE OF
    `no: an appeal does not lie from a decision about the transfer or removal of a proceeding under this Act (s 13(a))`
    `no: an appeal does not lie from a decision on which rules of evidence and procedure apply (s 13(b))`
    `s 13 does not bar it; whether an appeal lies is for the general law and other sections (s 7 on routes)`

GIVEN `decision` IS A `Decision`
GIVETH AN `Appeal bar answer`
`can the decision be appealed (s 13)` `decision` MEANS
    CONSIDER `decision`
    WHEN `a decision to transfer or remove the proceeding, or not to, under this Act` THEN `no: an appeal does not lie from a decision about the transfer or removal of a proceeding under this Act (s 13(a))`
    WHEN `a decision on which rules of evidence and procedure apply under s 11(1)` THEN `no: an appeal does not lie from a decision on which rules of evidence and procedure apply (s 13(b))`
    OTHERWISE `s 13 does not bar it; whether an appeal lies is for the general law and other sections (s 7 on routes)`

DECLARE `Enforcement answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: it is enforceable in South Australia as if given entirely in the Supreme Court's own jurisdiction (s 14(2))`
    `s 14(2) is not needed: a Supreme Court of South Australia judgment given in its own jurisdiction is enforceable as such`
    `not covered: s 14(2) speaks only of judgments of the Supreme Court of South Australia`

GIVEN `SA Supreme Court judgment` IS A BOOLEAN, `given in cross-vested jurisdiction` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH AN `Enforcement answer`
`is the judgment enforceable in South Australia (s 14(2))` `SA Supreme Court judgment` `given in cross-vested jurisdiction` MEANS
    BRANCH IF (NOT `SA Supreme Court judgment`) THEN `not covered: s 14(2) speaks only of judgments of the Supreme Court of South Australia`
           IF `given in cross-vested jurisdiction` THEN `yes: it is enforceable in South Australia as if given entirely in the Supreme Court's own jurisdiction (s 14(2))`
           OTHERWISE `s 14(2) is not needed: a Supreme Court of South Australia judgment given in its own jurisdiction is enforceable as such`

DECLARE `Reference answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the South Australian law's reference to the Supreme Court is read as a reference to the court that did it (s 14(3))`
    `not under s 14(3): it excludes laws relating to the enforcement of judgments (s 14(2) deals with enforcement)`
    `s 14(3) does not apply: the thing was not done by another court in jurisdiction conferred by this Act`

GIVEN `done by another court under this Act` IS A BOOLEAN, `an enforcement law` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Reference answer`
`does South Australian law treat it as done by the Supreme Court (s 14(3))` `done by another court under this Act` `an enforcement law` MEANS
    BRANCH IF (NOT `done by another court under this Act`) THEN `s 14(3) does not apply: the thing was not done by another court in jurisdiction conferred by this Act`
           IF `an enforcement law` THEN `not under s 14(3): it excludes laws relating to the enforcement of judgments (s 14(2) deals with enforcement)`
           OTHERWISE `yes: the South Australian law's reference to the Supreme Court is read as a reference to the court that did it (s 14(3))`
